  "response": "\"자는 시간이 아니라 확인하는 시간\"이라고 표현해 주신 게 정말 정확하네요. 누워서 쉬는 게 아니라, 낮에 있었던 일을 밤에 다시 꺼내서 분석하고 있는 거니까요. 그건 잠자리가 아니라 일하는 자리인 셈이죠.\n\n그리고 처음에 이유를 모르겠다고 하셨는데, 지금 말씀해 주신 걸 보면 패턴이 꽤 선명해요. 그냥 잠이 안 오는 게 아니라, 사람들과의 대화 내용을 되짚는 생각들이 잠을 막고 있는 거잖아요.\n\n그 되짚는 대화들이 혹시 특정 사람이나 특정 상황과 관련이 있는 건가요, 아니면 낮에 있었던 거의 모든 대화가 다 그런 식으로 올라오는 건가요?",
  "reasoning": "The client has shared a very vivid and specific description of their sleep difficulty. They're describing rumination - replaying conversations, questioning what people meant, worrying about how their own words were received. This is a pattern of social anxiety-related rumination that's interfering with sleep.\n\nKey observations:\n- The client said \"이유를 잘 모르겠고\" earlier, but now they've actually shown a very clear pattern - it's not random insomnia, it's rumination about social interactions\n- They used the phrase \"자는 시간이 아니라 계속 확인하는 시간\" which is a powerful self-observation\n- They're not just having trouble sleeping - they're mentally rehearsing/analyzing social interactions at night\n- This suggests social anxiety or high interpersonal sensitivity\n\nWhat should I do next?\n- I should reflect back what they've described, especially their own powerful phrase about \"확인하는 시간\"\n- I could gently note that they said they didn't know why, but they've actually described something quite specific - the content of the rumination is about social interactions\n- I should be careful not to diagnose or assume social anxiety\n- I could ask about the content of these thoughts - are there specific relationships or situations that trigger this, or is it general?\n- I should validate that this is exhausting - lying in bed doing mental \"verification\" work is draining\n\nI'll reflect their own insight, gently point out that they've actually described a pattern (even though they said they didn't know why), and ask something that helps explore the content further without being too directive.\n",
